WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.895 --> 00:00:06.270
RADHA MATHUR:
Hello, and welcome.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.370
I'm Radha Mathur.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.260
I'm a product manager
at Google Cloud.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.290
And in this session, I will be
presenting an overview of HA

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.870
and data protection on GCP.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.690
We'll go over the catalog
of HA and data protection

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:23.330
capabilities for both IaaS and
PaaS workloads running on GCP.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.240
And I'm happy to share that I
will be joined by Jeremy Tinley

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.730
from Etsy, who has
graciously agreed

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.119
to describe how Etsy uses
and benefits from some

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.480
of these capabilities.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.060
Let's get started.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.080
HA and data protection
solutions are

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.980
required to maintain
business continuity.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.060
Key business
continuity objectives

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.420
are to guard against
accidental data loss

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.020
due to user errors, data
corruption, or ransomware,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.140
and to achieve resilience
to infrastructure failures,

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.720
such as zone failures,
competence failures,

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:57.670
or even failures
of entire regions

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.810
that could be caused
by natural disasters.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.660
Common HA and data
protection solutions

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.300
that enable business
continuity include

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.170
backups, cross-zone
high availability,

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:14.430
and cross-region replication
and DR orchestration.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.090
These solutions form a
spectrum with respect

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:22.110
to the level of resiliency that
they provide and their cost.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.680
And the choice of any one
solution for a workload

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.740
depends on the business
criticality of the workload

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.250
as well as the tolerance of
that workload to downtime.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:36.870
For example, workloads that can
tolerate a higher recovery time

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.390
could employ backups
for protection

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:43.335
against both user errors
and infrastructure failures.

00:01:43.335 --> 00:01:46.080
Whereas mission
critical workloads

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.590
that have stringent
uptime requirements

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.420
would typically use
replication-based solutions

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:53.850
for protection from
infrastructure failures,

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.700
and in addition to that
use backups for protection

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.390
from user errors.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:01.970
Now, irrespective of where your
workload is in this spectrum,

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:07.020
our goal at GCP is to provide
a rich catalog of built-in data

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.590
protection primitives as
well as ISP partner products

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.540
to help design a
business continuity

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.320
solution that is aligned with
the needs of your business.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.830
GCP Compute, Storage,
and many PaaS services

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.860
offer a wide range of
replication and backup

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.030
capabilities.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.790
These primitives serve
as building blocks

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.360
that can help you form a
business continuity solution.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.290
In addition to that, we have a
rich ecosystem of ISP partner

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.400
products that augment the
primitives available on GCP

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.040
and support a wide
variety of use cases,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.460
from protecting GCE VMs,
to enterprise applications

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.850
such as SAP, to
replicating entire VMware

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:54.130
environments from on-premises
to GCP for disaster recovery.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.900
Various protection
mechanisms can

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.620
be employed for protecting
application workloads running

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.090
on GCP-managed infrastructure.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.200
Let's take a deeper look
at some of these HA data

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.060
protection primitives.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:10.720
To achieve high availability
in the event of infrastructure

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.150
unavailability such
as zone failure,

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.360
VMs and Containers
can be distributed

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.530
across zones in a region.

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:22.240
Similarly, both
persistent disk and GCS

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.570
offer regional instances
that replicate data

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.850
across zones in a region.

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.750
Capabilities such as VM machine
images and persistent disk

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:36.850
snapshots enabling backing up
of both VM configuration as well

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:38.770
as application data.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.230
And similar backup
capabilities are

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.860
in the pipeline for Anthos
GKE Clusters as well

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.896
as Cloud Filestore.

00:03:46.896 --> 00:03:51.160
Georedundant Cloud Storage
skews help replicate data

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.220
across regions for
disaster recovery.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.130
And DR orchestration is
enabled by Deployment Manager.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.890
Now, in addition to compute
and storage primitives,

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.400
we're adding primitives
in the near future

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.750
to aid in application-consistent
backups such as persistent disk

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:14.150
app-consistent snapshot hooks
and SAP-certified backint

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.600
agents for Google Cloud Storage.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.019
GCP PaaS databases and
analytics services also

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:23.720
pack in a rich set of
replication and backup

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.040
capabilities.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.220
Managed databases such as Cloud
Spanner and Cloud Bigtable

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.250
offer modes for cross-zone
and cross-region replication.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.420
Cloud SQL HA instances
and Google BigQuery

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.180
regional instances
provide high availability

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.220
against zone failures.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:44.420
For data backups, PaaS databases
on GCP offer primitives

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.830
such as managed backups as well
as the ability to export data

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:51.370
in industry standard formats.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:56.030
Cloud SQL also enables granular
recovery from user errors

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:00.390
or application corruption
via a point-in-time recovery.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.660
Having looked at primitives
suited for IaaS and PaaS

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.120
workloads running
on GCP, let's look

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.110
at how one of our customers is
using GCP's business continuity

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:15.280
primitives as building blocks in
their data protection strategy.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:18.310
I'm pleased to welcome
Jeremy Tinley, senior staff

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.670
engineer on Etsy's database
infrastructure team,

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.560
to share insights
from their production

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.660
strategy for etsy.com.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.460
Welcome, Jeremy.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:29.020
JEREMY TINLEY: Thanks, Radha.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:31.410
I've been at Etsy for six
years, and over that time,

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:34.120
the infrastructure has
considerably evolved.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.715
We recently migrated
to Google Cloud,

00:05:35.715 --> 00:05:37.840
and this gave us a chance
to rethink our general DR

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.130
strategy.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.790
Google Cloud offers a wide range
of services for DR. Selecting

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:46.010
and, more importantly, adopting
them was very important to us.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:49.230
But first, a little
bit about Etsy.

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:51.210
Etsy is a global
online marketplace

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.840
where people come together to
make, sell, buy, and collect

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.410
unique items.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.300
So who sells on Etsy?

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.030
Etsy sellers are people
from all over the world.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:03.300
They use their incredible
talent to make things.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.490
This can be blankets made in
Morocco, jewelry from Ecuador,

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:09.123
or even soap made in Seattle.

00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:11.040
They can also be people
who have vintage items

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.540
that they're finally
willing to part with.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:17.100
On the flip side of the
marketplace, you have buyers.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.930
Etsy buyers browse
the marketplace

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.440
looking for items based
on personal style or need.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:25.110
My current favorite is buying
handmade chocolate truffles.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.450
I usually buy them
locally made from somebody

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:30.060
who's in my community,
who's my neighbor.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:33.748
And that also helps because
the shipping is really quick.

00:06:33.748 --> 00:06:35.290
My favorite thing
about Etsy, though,

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:37.920
is that they enable a
human-to-human connection.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.840
You're not buying
something from a warehouse.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.880
You're buying it
from a human being.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:44.340
Working at Etsy
is a bit similar.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.440
Humans are crafting
infrastructure and code.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.930
Hence our phrase, code is craft.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:52.170
With that, let's review
our architecture.

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:56.040
Etsy is a traditional LAMP stack
consisting of Linux, Apache,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.170
MySQL, and PHP.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.213
Since Etsy is an
e-commerce marketplace,

00:07:00.213 --> 00:07:02.130
you can probably imagine
a typical interaction

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.250
with the website.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.250
A seller lists new
items by entering

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:09.270
in the name, the
pricing, and description.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.358
Buyers browsing Etsy get to
read the seller's content.

00:07:12.358 --> 00:07:14.400
They can initiate a
conversation with the seller,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:16.980
asking them questions
about their products.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:20.692
They add an item to their cart,
and hopefully, they purchase.

00:07:20.692 --> 00:07:22.150
After they've made
a purchase, they

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:23.775
can track the shipping status.

00:07:23.775 --> 00:07:25.150
And when they
receive their item,

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:28.413
they can leave ratings and
feedback for the store.

00:07:28.413 --> 00:07:30.080
Both the front-end
and back-end services

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.478
run on Google Compute Engine
VMs, not on a hosted platform.

00:07:34.478 --> 00:07:36.020
We do this because
we want more hands

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.740
on control over the systems.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.840
On the front-end, we use
managed instance groups

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.020
to maintain availability
of web and API servers.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.690
This makes it trivial to
add or remove capacity,

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:49.007
and failing servers are
depooled automatically.

00:07:49.007 --> 00:07:50.590
Our front-end workloads
are stateless,

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:52.382
and this makes it
straightforward to manage

00:07:52.382 --> 00:07:54.142
disaster recovery.

00:07:54.142 --> 00:07:55.600
Moving to the
back-end, however, is

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:57.790
where things get complicated.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.290
From the description
above, you can

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:02.770
surmise that we run a
traditional OLTP workload.

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.410
All of the data that buyers and
sellers generate and interact

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.110
with is stored in MySQL.

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:10.270
From a DR point of
view, we primarily

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:14.680
rely on MySQL native replication
for multiple copies of data.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.890
We execute periodic snapshots
of our data, and for each one,

00:08:17.890 --> 00:08:20.510
we run a restore test
process to validate it.

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:23.740
For transaction logs, we sync
those to Google Cloud Storage

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.490
buckets on a scheduled basis.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:29.410
However, our world wasn't
always so wonderful.

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:31.625
Etsy is 15 years old,
and for most of that,

00:08:31.625 --> 00:08:34.000
it was hosted in a traditional
datacenter with bare metal

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.320
servers.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.850
The good news about running
your own data center--

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.320
you can manage everything.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:42.440
The bad news-- you have
to manage everything.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.380
So let's look at one
specific case dealing

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:45.970
with hardware failure.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:48.490
I think we can all agree that
computers are both amazing

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:50.590
and also terrible.

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.698
Hardware failures,
especially disks, happen.

00:08:53.698 --> 00:08:55.990
This meant that we needed to
be able to rebuild servers

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:57.162
as the need arose.

00:08:57.162 --> 00:08:59.620
This was especially painful
when dealing with large amounts

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.600
of data in MySQL.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.570
The first step was just to
provision a bare metal server.

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:06.760
On a good day, an
hour was optimistic.

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.090
On a bad day, we would fight
with UEFI settings, boot disk

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:14.180
issues, and Anaconda
was not a good friend.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:16.710
Let's assume that all that
goes correctly, though.

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.530
After we've built our
bare mental server,

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:20.870
we have to restore MySQL data.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:22.460
We copy our data
over the network,

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:25.970
decrypt and decompress it, bring
a database online, and wait

00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:27.705
for replication to catch up.

00:09:27.705 --> 00:09:29.330
Although it was all
automated, it still

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.100
took a long time to perform--

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.230
many hours.

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:36.200
This same slow down translated
to our backup process.

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.170
We ran into a longer than
desired RTO, or recovery

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:40.580
time objective.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:43.730
Our MySQL data backups
were being performed daily.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.770
Backups took about
three hours to complete,

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.500
which for us was painfully slow.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.320
These backups were full
backups sent over a network

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:53.210
to specific backup
servers that had

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:55.050
lots of disk loaded in them.

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:57.320
However, the process
doesn't end there.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.600
As the old adage goes,
a backup isn't a backup

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.500
until it's been tested.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.900
Once our backup was transferred,
a restore test process

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.230
was kicked off to verify that
the data could be recovered.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.690
This involved once again
copying the full backup

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:12.450
over the network to
restore testing server,

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.720
decrypting and decompressing,
bringing the database online,

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.910
and waiting for
replication to catch up.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.410
As I said before,
that whole process

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:22.680
took multiple hours to achieve.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.580
When these problems
are magnified at scale,

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:26.830
both for the amount of
data and copies of data,

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:28.910
it begins to stress
the infrastructure.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.360
Etsy ran many, many
copies of data,

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:33.160
each with a different purpose.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:36.620
We have our main production
workload that has redundancy.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:38.680
We have geographic redundancy.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:40.720
And others were
artificially delayed

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:43.330
to allow faster online
recovery in the event

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.720
of an inadvertent data change.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:51.640
Thankfully, late in 2018, we
migrated Etsy to Google Cloud.

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:53.470
In our relatively
new cloud world,

00:10:53.470 --> 00:10:55.900
we found easier solutions
to these problems.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:57.760
As I said earlier,
the front-end takes

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.260
advantage of managed
instance groups

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.290
to improve the speed at which
failed nodes are replaced.

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:03.250
The host is failed.

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:04.510
The host is destroyed.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:07.450
And it's rebuilt. The host is
added back to production when

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:09.030
it's registered as healthy.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:11.470
Before this magic, disks
would have to be replaced.

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:14.450
Hosts would have to
be rebuilt by hand.

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:17.030
No one even has to pay attention
to these issues anymore.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:21.330
This frees them up to focus
on other areas of improvement.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:22.940
Our new MySQL
environment was designed

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.730
to take advantage of native
Google Cloud features.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:28.190
The big shift was to use
persistent disks instead

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:29.660
of local disks.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.790
We built our databases
with one boot disk

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:35.780
for logs and state data, like
PID files and lock files.

00:11:35.780 --> 00:11:38.730
The second disk is
only for MySQL data.

00:11:38.730 --> 00:11:41.780
Performing a live disk
snapshot in MySQL is--

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:44.105
no pun intended-- a snap.

00:11:44.105 --> 00:11:45.980
There's no need to
quiesce a database either.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:48.340
When MySQL starts up,
it's treated like a crash,

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:50.030
and it recovers itself.

00:11:50.030 --> 00:11:53.300
By using persistent disks,
we got very easy differential

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.040
backups with PD snapshots.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:57.230
And coming from the world
where we did full backups

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:59.930
every single day, this improved
our backup times from two

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:02.250
to three hours down
to three minutes.

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:05.420
We can now take many snapshots
per day, giving us the ability

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:09.200
to roll back data to
previously known good point.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:12.210
This eliminated the need
for an online delayed copy.

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:14.520
Also, our snapshots
are multi-region,

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.860
so we no longer have to
manage shipping full backups

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:19.810
across the entire US.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:21.790
Or restore test
process is similar

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:24.430
as it was before,
with some wins.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:27.560
After we obtain a snapshot,
we again do a restore test.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:30.970
However, this time in GCP we're
able to provision a new VM

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.320
dynamically.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:35.140
We create and attach a
new disk from a snapshot.

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:38.450
We bring MySQL fully online
and have it replicating.

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:40.060
By taking more
frequent snapshots,

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.940
replication catch up time
was significantly reduced.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:44.930
This process used
to run for hours.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.040
We can now test a copy of
data in just 15 minutes.

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:52.440
Another big win was to
use Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:53.990
We consistently
send our transaction

00:12:53.990 --> 00:12:57.080
logs to a bucket with
Lifecycle Management enabled.

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:59.090
This helps us maintain
a retention policy that

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.790
makes sense for our use case,
including automatic purging

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:03.620
of files when they age off.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:07.300
And let me tell you, legal is
really happy with that one.

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:08.850
Our buckets are
also multi-regional,

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:11.370
giving us geographic
redundancy with our backups,

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:13.020
just like our snapshots.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:16.680
All of this translates to
stress-free disaster recovery.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.620
Recapping our results, we saw a
big benefit with our migration

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:20.850
to Google Cloud.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:23.940
Front-end server builds are
much faster, and automatic.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:27.582
On the back-end, we see a
tremendous improvement in RTO,

00:13:27.582 --> 00:13:29.290
both the speed at
which we can provision,

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:31.897
down to how quickly the
data can be brought online.

00:13:31.897 --> 00:13:34.480
I think we've just scratched the
surface with what's possible.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:35.938
We plan on looking
at additional DR

00:13:35.938 --> 00:13:39.307
services that can be leveraged
to make our life easier.

00:13:39.307 --> 00:13:40.890
These results might
have been possible

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:42.540
in a traditional
datacenter, but it

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:44.730
would require additional
staffing, infrastructure,

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:46.175
and capacity management.

00:13:46.175 --> 00:13:48.300
As someone who participates
in an on-call rotation,

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.550
I'm quite happy to offload those
challenges to Google Cloud.

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:54.270
RADHA MATHUR: Thanks, Jeremy,
for the insightful description

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:57.150
of the impact you've seen by
use of HA and data protection

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.970
primitives as part
of your GCP stack.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:03.330
From backup of data disks,
to replication of transaction

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:07.350
logs, to use of highly-available
managed instance groups for web

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:09.900
servers, the solution
shared by Jeremy

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:13.290
is representative of how
primitives across the spectrum

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:16.260
can be used for different
components of your workload

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:20.300
based on the needs
of your business.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:23.440
This concludes our overview
of HA and data protection

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:24.910
capabilities on GCP.

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.450
Thank you for joining us.

